Capacitors help maintain stable power supplies and support reliable operation of electronic components.

A 0.1 µF capacitor is commonly placed close to an IC's supply pins to provide a local path for high-frequency transient currents.

Larger capacitors, such as 10 µF and 22 µF, can provide local energy storage and reduce supply-voltage variations.

The actual purpose of each capacitor depends on its position in the schematic. Capacitor voltage ratings, dielectric types, and placement must be verified before fabrication.

