6. Top Copper-Layer View

The drafting document includes a top copper-layer view of the PCB.

The copper layer contains conductive tracks and copper features used to connect electronic components.

The routing pattern represents the physical implementation of electrical connections defined by the circuit design.

Main purposes

Electrical interconnection: Copper tracks connect component pads according to the PCB net assignments.

Signal routing: Tracks provide paths for communication and control signals between the controller and peripherals.

Power distribution: Appropriately sized tracks and copper regions distribute supply voltages to connected components.

Routing organization: The PCB layout organizes electrical connections within the available board area.

Important design considerations
Maintain the required clearance between different electrical nets.
Select trace widths according to current and thermal requirements.
Avoid unnecessary routing around sensitive signals.
Follow appropriate layout practices for switching regulators.
Check the continuity of ground and power connections.
Verify that no unintended copper connections exist.

The copper-layer image provides a visual representation of the routing, but detailed electrical verification must be performed in the PCB design software.

