9.1 Plated holes

A plated hole has a conductive metal layer on its inner surface.

Depending on the PCB design, plated holes can be used for:

Vias connecting different copper layers.
Through-hole component connections.
Other electrical interconnections between layers.

The final use of each hole depends on its pad, via, and net assignments in the PCB design.

9.2 Non-plated holes

A non-plated hole does not have a conductive metal lining.

Non-plated holes are commonly used for mechanical purposes, such as mounting, alignment, or fixing the PCB to a structure.

The two 0.65 mm non-plated holes shown in the table should be checked against the PCB design to confirm their intended mechanical function.

