Drill symbols and their corresponding locations.
Mounting-hole dimensions.
Via and annular-ring requirements.
Compatibility with the manufacturer's fabrication capabilities.

The drill table should be generated from the final PCB design so that it matches the manufacturing output.

10. Relationship Between the BOM, PCB Layout, and 3D Model

The BOM, PCB layout, and 3D model provide different but complementary information.

Design document	Main purpose
Schematic	Defines electrical connections and circuit functionality
PCB layout	Defines component placement and copper routing
BOM	Lists components and quantities required for assembly
Top-side view	Shows component positions and orientations
Copper-layer view	Shows conductive tracks and copper features
3D model	Shows the physical arrangement of the assembled PCB
Drill table	Documents hole sizes, quantities, and plating information

All these documents must agree with one another.

For example, a component listed in the BOM must have a corresponding footprint in the PCB design, and that footprint must match the physical component selected for assembly.

Similarly, the drill table and manufacturing files must correspond to the final PCB layout.

Maintaining consistency between these documents reduces the risk of incorrect components, unsuitable footprints, and manufacturing errors.

11. Manufacturing Preparation

Before sending the PCB for fabrication, the design should be reviewed carefully.

11.1 Component verification
Confirm the component part numbers.
Check component footprints against datasheets.
Verify component orientations and pin numbering.
Confirm connector pin assignments.
Check capacitor voltage ratings and resistor values.
Verify that the selected components are compatible with their intended circuits.
11.2 PCB verification
Run the Design Rule Check (DRC).
Check track clearances and trace widths.
Verify copper-to-board-edge clearances.
Check the board outline and mounting holes.
Review the ground and power connections.
Inspect the placement of sensitive components.
Check the switching-regulator layout.
Confirm that the footprints match the selected components.
11.3 Manufacturing-file verification

Generate the required manufacturing files from the final PCB design.

Depending on the manufacturer, these may include:

Gerber files for the copper, solder-mask, silkscreen, and board-outline layers.
NC drill files for holes and vias.
Fabrication notes.
Bill of Materials.
Pick-and-place data, if required for assembly.

Inspect the generated files before submission to ensure that they represent the intended final design.

11.4 Assembly verification

After fabrication and assembly:

Inspect the PCB for visible manufacturing defects.
Check component orientation and soldering quality.
Verify that there are no unintended shorts between supply rails.
Apply power using an appropriate current-limited supply.
Measure the required supply voltages.
Verify controller programming and debugging interfaces.
Test the sensors and communication interfaces individually.
Perform system-level testing after the individual circuits have been verified.

The PCB should not be considered fully validated until electrical and functional tests have been completed successfully.

